| **Olmec** | Settled in the humid, tropical lowlands of Mexico's Gulf Coast. River floods deposited rich silt on plains, enabling fertile maize and crop agriculture.

 | Ruled by powerful priest-kings who controlled trade routes and directed public labor for massive building projects.

 | Centered on shamanistic polytheism and jaguar deities; guided agricultural cycles, daily rituals, and elite authority.

 | Evolved into a hierarchy topped by priest-rulers and elite artisans, supported by a base of peasant farmers.

 | Known as Mesoamerica's "Mother Culture"; pioneered colossal stone heads, early drainage systems, ballgames, and calendars.

 |
| **Aztecs** | Built their capital in the high Valley of Mexico on Lake Texcoco; created *chinampas* (floating gardens) and aqueducts to adapt to swampy terrain.

 | Centralized empire led by the emperor (*Tlatoani*) and nobles. Enforced strict legal codes, taxation, and universal military training.

 | Polytheistic state religion dominated daily life; required sacrifices to sun and rain gods (Huitzilopochtli, Tlaloc) to preserve cosmic balance.

 | Rigid hierarchy: divine emperor and nobles (*pipiltin*) at the top, followed by merchants (*pochteca*), commoners (*macehualtin*), and slaves.

 | Developed advanced hydraulic engineering, universal education, accurate dual-calendar systems, and herbal medicine.

 |
| **Maya** | Occupied rainforests and limestone lowlands across Southern Mexico and Central America; used raised fields, terraces, and water reservoirs.

 | Organized into independent city-states (e.g., Tikal, Palenque) ruled by divine hereditary kings (*K'uhul Ajaw*) through local administrators.

 | Deeply integrated into daily life; priests performed bloodletting and offerings to rain, sun, and maize deities to ensure prosperity.

 | Highly stratified: divine royal family and priests held ultimate power, followed by specialized artisans/merchants, farmers, and slaves.

 | Invented a complete hieroglyphic writing system, the mathematical concept of zero, precise astronomical calendars, and grand stone pyramids.

 |
| **Inca** | Mastered the rugged Andean mountains of South America; engineered stone agricultural terraces, irrigation canals, and high-altitude food storage.

 | Highly centralized empire (*Tawantinsuyu*) ruled by the *Sapa Inca*. Regulated daily life via the *mita* mandatory labor system and food redistribution.

 | Centered on Inti (Sun God) and ancestor worship. Religion scheduled agricultural activities, public festivals, and empire-wide obligations.

 | Hierarchical pyramid led by the Sapa Inca and royal clans, followed by provincial nobles (*curacas*), and common *ayllu* agricultural workers.

 | Constructed 25,000+ miles of paved mountain roads, mortarless earthquake-resistant masonry, terrace farming, and *quipu* record-keeping.

